{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LnGtdZdYATYouFYEyp1xA6","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Scores**: Soundness 3.0, Excitement 3.5, Overall 3.0 (Findings acceptance), Confidence 4/5.\n\n**Core assessment**: The Embedding Alignment Gap is a genuinely novel phenomenon with a clean experimental design and compelling main result. The selection ratio metric is well-conceived, and the three-way comparison provides good causal attribution.\n\n**Primary concerns raised**:\n\n1. **Narrow scale range** -- 125M and 355M within the same GPT-2 family is insufficient to claim \"scale invariance.\" The near-identical relative magnitudes (51.4% vs 51.9%) are suspiciously precise.\n\n2. **Re-anchoring lacks multi-seed validation** -- The most actionable contribution (plan_008) uses only seed=42, undermining the 1/3 vs 2/3 decomposition claim.\n\n3. **Extreme self-training setup** -- Student-as-generator with no real data mixing is the most aggressive collapse configuration. Real-world applicability is unclear.\n\n4. **Code-paper discrepancies** -- The rank correlation metric description in the paper (embedding diversity vs. rare n-gram rank) differs from the code implementation (internal vs. Contriever L2-to-centroid). The rare n-gram definition also has a threshold discrepancy (<=5 count vs. 10th percentile).\n\n5. **Missing hyperparameters** -- Generation token count, selection budget, temperature, learning rate, PCA dimensionality, and training epochs are not specified in the paper.\n\n6. **N=3 statistical limitations** -- CIs are technically correct but CI coverage probability with 2 degrees of freedom is unreliable.\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_4/projects/phase-gated-compound-sparse/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T081019/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a91e57ee182587b33 (use SendMessage with to: 'a91e57ee182587b33' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 98879\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 173082</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a73def8-44d6-41cf-95fb-f642ea1414d1","uuid":"94c62a8d-d39d-4f15-b055-76bd61184fd1","timestamp":"2026-05-13T08:50:39.051Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an ACL Rolling Review (ARR) submission targeting EMNLP 2026. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Produce a blind, independent review.\n\n## ARR Scoring Scales\n- **Soundness** (1-5, 0.5 increments): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major Issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5, 0.5 increments): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially Interesting, 1=Not Exciting\n- **Overall** (1-5, 0.5 increments): 5=Award, 4.5=Borderline Award, 4=Conference acceptance, 3.5=Borderline Conference, 3=Findings acceptance, 2.5=Borderline Findings, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Confidence** (1-5): 5=Positive, 4=Quite sure, 3=Pretty sure, 2=Willing to defend, 1=Not my area\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"The Embedding Alignment Gap: Why Diversity-Based Data Selection Accelerates Model Collapse in Iterative Self-Training\"\n\nThe paper identifies the \"Embedding Alignment Gap\" — in iterative self-training loops, a language model's internal embeddings progressively become misaligned with content diversity, causing diversity-based data selection (RGDS) to *accelerate* model collapse rather than prevent it.\n\n**Key Claims:**\n- C1: Selection ratio degrades monotonically from 1.04→0.52 across 8 generations\n- C2-weak: Frozen external embeddings (Contriever) maintain ratio >1.0 throughout\n- C3: RGDS produces +50% PPL and -47% rare n-gram vs uncurated baseline\n- S1: Semantic diversity ≠ lexical diversity (external improves PPL but not rare n-grams)\n- S2: RankMe/k-NN metrics fail to detect the collapse\n\n**Method (§3):**\n- Iterative self-training loop: Generate → Select → Train for 8 generations\n- Three selection strategies: Uncurated (no selection), RGDS (internal embedding facility-location), External-only (frozen Contriever)\n- Selection ratio metric: avg-rare(selected) / avg-rare(pool)\n- Evaluation: PPL, rare n-gram survival, rank correlation, RankMe, k-NN\n\n**Experiments (§4-7):**\n- §4: GPT2-125M, WikiText-103, 3 seeds (42,123,456). RGDS gen-7: PPL=72.5±1.9, ratio=0.517±0.025, rare=0.0046±0.0004. Uncurated gen-7: PPL=48.3±0.9, rare=0.0088. CIs non-overlapping, Cohen's d>3.\n- §5: External-only gen-7: PPL=45.7±0.8 (better than uncurated), rare=0.0088 (identical to uncurated). Selection ratio=1.28±0.03 (CI above 1.0).\n- §6: Re-anchoring (use M0's embeddings for selection): ratio restored 0.52→0.95, PPL 72.8→51.2 (-29.7%), rare 0.0046→0.0061 (+32.6%). Decomposes collapse into selection-side (~1/3) and generation-side (~2/3).\n- §7.1: 3-seed aggregation with 95% CIs. All key contrasts hold.\n- §7.2: GPT2-medium 355M (2 seeds). Relative RGDS PPL excess 51.9% (vs 51.4% at 125M). Scale-invariant.\n- §7.3: OpenWebText dataset. RGDS PPL excess +53.5% (vs +51.4% WikiText). Dataset-invariant.\n\n**Tables:**\n- Table 1: Selection ratio across 8 generations (seed=42)\n- Table 2: Core metrics at gen 0 and gen 7 (3-seed mean±std)\n- Table 3: RankMe/k-NN under RGDS collapse\n- Table 4: Re-anchoring results\n- Table 5: 3-seed aggregated statistics with CIs\n- Table 6: Cross-scale comparison (125M vs 355M)\n- Table 7: Cross-dataset comparison (WikiText vs OpenWebText)\n\n**Reference list** (9 citations): Shumailov 2024 (Nature), Alemohammad 2024, Dohmatob 2024, Abbas 2023 (SemDeDup), Tirumala 2023 (D4), Xie 2023 (DSIR), Papyan 2020 (Neural Collapse), Garrido 2023 (RankMe), Izacard 2022 (Contriever), Kirchhoff & Bilmes 2014.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from experimental plan)\n\nThe full planned experiment suite includes:\n1. Main 3-method comparison: GPT2-125M × WikiText-103 × 8 gens × 3 methods (plan_001, seed=42)\n2. Seed=123 full reproduction (plan_004)\n3. Seed=456 full reproduction + 3-seed aggregation with 95% CIs (plan_005)\n4. GPT2-medium 355M × 3 methods (plan_006, seed=42)\n5. GPT2-medium 355M × seed=123 reproduction (plan_007)\n6. Embedding re-anchoring mitigation (plan_008)\n7. OpenWebText cross-dataset validation (plan_009, seed=42)\n\nAll 7 experiments appear to be reflected in the paper.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions in depth:\n\n1. **Experimental Design**: Is the setup appropriate for the claims? Are baselines sufficient? Is the self-training loop realistic?\n2. **Statistical Rigor**: 3 seeds with 95% CIs. Is N=3 sufficient? Are effect sizes adequate? Is the statistical methodology appropriate?\n3. **Metrics**: Is \"selection ratio\" a well-motivated metric? Is rare n-gram survival appropriate? Are PPL evaluations sound?\n4. **Scale and Generalization**: GPT2-125M and 355M only. Two datasets. Is this sufficient for the claims made?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Are enough details provided? Hyperparameters, generation details, selection subset sizes?\n6. **Missing Experiments**: What experiments are missing that would strengthen the paper?\n7. **Re-anchoring Mitigation**: Is this experiment well-designed? Is the interpretation of the selection-side vs generation-side decomposition valid?\n\nProduce your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Paper Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments / Suggestions\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Justification\n[1 paragraph justifying scores]\n```\n","agentId":"a91e57ee182587b33","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Scores**: Soundness 3.0, Excitement 3.5, Overall 3.0 (Findings acceptance), Confidence 4/5.\n\n**Core assessment**: The Embedding Alignment Gap is a genuinely novel phenomenon with a clean experimental design and compelling main result. The selection ratio metric is well-conceived, and the three-way comparison provides good causal attribution.\n\n**Primary concerns raised**:\n\n1. **Narrow scale range** -- 125M and 355M within the same GPT-2 family is insufficient to claim \"scale invariance.\" The near-identical relative magnitudes (51.4% vs 51.9%) are suspiciously precise.\n\n2. **Re-anchoring lacks multi-seed validation** -- The most actionable contribution (plan_008) uses only seed=42, undermining the 1/3 vs 2/3 decomposition claim.\n\n3. **Extreme self-training setup** -- Student-as-generator with no real data mixing is the most aggressive collapse configuration. Real-world applicability is unclear.\n\n4. **Code-paper discrepancies** -- The rank correlation metric description in the paper (embedding diversity vs. rare n-gram rank) differs from the code implementation (internal vs. Contriever L2-to-centroid). The rare n-gram definition also has a threshold discrepancy (<=5 count vs. 10th percentile).\n\n5. **Missing hyperparameters** -- Generation token count, selection budget, temperature, learning rate, PCA dimensionality, and training epochs are not specified in the paper.\n\n6. **N=3 statistical limitations** -- CIs are technically correct but CI coverage probability with 2 degrees of freedom is unreliable.\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_4/projects/phase-gated-compound-sparse/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T081019/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":173082,"totalTokens":98879,"totalToolUseCount":25,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3149,"cache_read_input_tokens":95268,"output_tokens":461,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3149},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":461,"cache_read_input_tokens":95268,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3149,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3149,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":16,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":5}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a02c5ea6adc5cc1aa","tool_use_id":"toolu_01VK81W9kNp4nJVM3Zcv39zM","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":68832,"tool_uses":22,"duration_ms":195586},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"a009a587-8a00-4905-ba24-baf315d6e5fd","session_id":"7a73def8-44d6-41cf-95fb-f642ea1414d1"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YLSbo9BMKcDQJJjqqu8VGN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ssr53aapv9CEoybUYLwXYf","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Paper Summary\n\nThis paper identifies the \"Embedding Alignment Gap\" -- a phenomenon in which a language model's internal embeddings become progressively misaligned with content diversity during iterative self-training. The core finding is that diversity-based data selection using internal embeddings accelerates model collapse rather than preventing it, with the selection ratio degrading from 1.04 to 0.52 over 8 generations of GPT-2 self-training. Frozen external embeddings (Contriever) are shown to be immune to this misalignment, though they fail to preserve lexical diversity. A simple re-anchoring fix partially recovers the gap.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Genuinely counterintuitive and well-articulated finding.** The central observation -- that diversity-based selection using internal embeddings *accelerates* collapse -- is surprising and practically important. The paper clearly articulates the causal chain (embedding space contraction -> \"geometrically diverse\" = \"vocabulary-poor\" -> positive feedback loop). This is the kind of \"watch out\" empirical finding that EMNLP Findings values.\n\n2. **The selection ratio metric is a clean diagnostic contribution.** Defining r_t = avg-rare(selected) / avg-rare(pool) as a quantitative measure of alignment between geometric and content diversity is simple, interpretable, and reusable. The monotonic degradation from >1 to ~0.5 provides a crisp visualization of the alignment gap that future work can adopt.\n\n3. **The semantic-vs-lexical diversity dissociation (S1) is well-documented.** The finding that external selection improves PPL by ~5% without improving rare n-gram survival cleanly separates two axes of collapse (semantic coverage vs. lexical tail preservation). This decomposition is under-appreciated in the model collapse literature and worth highlighting.\n\n4. **The decomposition of collapse into selection-side vs generation-side components (Section 6) is analytically valuable.** Re-anchoring recovers most of the PPL gap but only ~33% of rare n-gram loss, localizing ~2/3 of vocabulary collapse to the generation side. This provides actionable direction for future mitigation work.\n\n5. **Robustness across seeds, scales, and datasets is above-average for this type of empirical study.** Three seeds with confidence intervals, two GPT-2 scales (125M, 355M), and two datasets (WikiText-103, OpenWebText) with consistent relative magnitudes is a solid experimental grid for a Findings paper.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **The related work section is severely underdeveloped for an EMNLP submission.** Only 10 references total (9 in-text + Kirchhoff & Bilmes 2014). For a paper that positions itself at the intersection of model collapse, data selection, and representation geometry, this is insufficient. Critical gaps are detailed below in \"Missing Related Work.\" At minimum, a Findings paper at EMNLP should engage with 25-40 references to demonstrate awareness of the landscape.\n\n2. **The novelty of the core finding is moderate, not high.** The idea that a model's internal representations become unreliable for self-assessment during iterative training is -- at a conceptual level -- somewhat expected. The model collapse literature already establishes that iterative self-training distorts the model's output distribution; that this distortion would extend to the embedding space used for diversity scoring follows logically. What *is* novel is the quantification (the selection ratio metric and its monotonic degradation), but the paper oversells the \"surprise\" element. The paper would benefit from a more honest framing: \"the extent and speed of the misalignment is surprising, even if the direction is not.\"\n\n3. **The connection to neural collapse (Papyan et al. 2020) is hand-wavy and may be misleading.** Neural collapse describes convergence of class means to a simplex ETF in the terminal phase of *supervised* classification training. The self-training loop here is unsupervised language modeling without discrete class structure. The paper draws an analogy (\"iterative self-training reduces intra-distribution variability\") but does not verify that the actual geometric signatures of neural collapse (NC1-NC4) are present. This analogy should be explicitly flagged as speculative, or removed if the authors cannot provide supporting evidence (e.g., measuring intra-class variability reduction or ETF-like convergence).\n\n4. **The \"best case\" numbers appear aspirational rather than grounded in current experimental evidence.** Cross-referencing with the project's experimental timeline: the actual completed experiments at the time of the dream report consist of one seed on GPT-2 125M with WikiText-103 for RGDS and uncurated (phase2_3method_selfit), with external_only still running through Gen 4. The 3-seed, 2-scale, 2-dataset results with tight confidence intervals in the \"best\" paper appear to be projections. If submitted, the authors must ensure all reported numbers come from completed experiments. The median paper's more modest claims (ratio 1.03->0.78, PPL +13%) with transient rebounds are more consistent with the available data and should be the basis for claims.\n\n5. **The paper does not adequately discuss embedding drift / concept drift.** The alignment gap is fundamentally an instance of concept drift applied to representation spaces. The machine learning literature on concept drift (Gama et al. 2014, Webb et al. 2016, Lu et al. 2019) and specifically embedding drift in deployed NLP systems (e.g., Lazaridou et al. 2021 on temporal concept drift) is directly relevant. The paper misses an opportunity to connect to this established framework, which would strengthen both the positioning and the terminology.\n\n6. **The S1 finding (semantic diversity != lexical diversity) is not novel.** The distinction between semantic and surface-level diversity has been recognized in data augmentation (Wei & Zou 2019), paraphrase generation, and corpus linguistics for decades. The Vendi Score paper (Friedman & Dieng 2023) explicitly discusses how different similarity kernels yield different diversity measurements. What is modestly novel is demonstrating this dissociation *in the specific context of iterative self-training*, but the paper presents it as if the general principle is new.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you verify that the monotonic degradation of the selection ratio (1.04 -> 0.52) holds across all 3 seeds, or do some seeds show transient rebounds (as the median paper version suggests)? The distinction between \"monotonic\" and \"overall declining with transient recovery\" is scientifically important and affects the mechanistic interpretation.\n\n2. The re-anchoring results (Table 6) appear to be single-seed (seed=42). Are multi-seed re-anchoring results available? Without them, the claimed 30% PPL recovery and 33% rare n-gram recovery lack statistical grounding.\n\n3. Why was Contriever chosen as the sole external embedding model? Have you tested with other frozen encoders (e.g., sentence-transformers, BGE, E5)? If the alignment gap is truly about internal vs. external embeddings, the choice of external encoder should not matter much -- but this should be verified.\n\n4. The selection budget ratio is 0.5 (selecting 50% of generated data). How sensitive is the alignment gap to this parameter? At lower selection ratios (e.g., 10%), the diversity pressure is much stronger and the gap could manifest differently.\n\n5. How does the selection ratio behave for uncurated training? By definition the uncurated method has no selection, but computing the \"hypothetical\" ratio (what ratio *would* RGDS produce if applied to uncurated's model at each generation without actually using the selection for training) would isolate whether the gap is caused by the selection feedback loop or by self-training dynamics alone.\n\n6. The paper claims the gap is \"a property of self-training dynamics, not of the data domain\" (Section 7.3). But the effect sizes are nearly identical across WikiText-103 and OpenWebText (PPL excess ~51-54%, ratio ~0.52-0.53). This uniformity is suspicious -- one would expect domain-dependent variation in how quickly the embedding space contracts. Can you comment on whether this reflects a genuine invariance or an artifact of the experimental setup?\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\n1. **Model collapse literature beyond the 3 cited papers:**\n   - Guo et al. (2024), \"Curious Decline of Linguistic Diversity\" -- directly studies lexical diversity loss in iterative training, closely related to the rare n-gram metric used here.\n   - Briesch et al. (2023), \"Large Language Models Suffer from Their Own Output\" -- early empirical characterization of self-training degradation.\n   - Hataya et al. (2023) on model collapse in diffusion models -- shows the phenomenon is modality-general.\n   - Dohmatob et al. (2024, ICML) \"Model Collapse Demystified\" -- provides tighter theoretical bounds.\n   - \"How to Synthesize Text Data without Model Collapse?\" (ToEdit, ICML 2025) -- directly relevant mitigation approach.\n   - \"Self-Verification Provably Prevents Model Collapse\" (NeurIPS 2025) -- the quality-based alternative to diversity-based selection.\n   - \"From Collapse to Improvement: Statistical Perspectives on Iterative Training\" (arXiv 2602.10531) -- recent theoretical work on when iterative training can *improve* models.\n\n2. **Data selection methods not cited:**\n   - LESS (Xia et al., 2024) -- gradient-based data selection, a non-embedding alternative.\n   - SelectIT (Liu et al., 2024) -- LLM-based data selection for instruction tuning.\n   - MONA (NeurIPS 2025) -- uses model-internal monosemantic neuronal activations for data selection; directly relevant since it demonstrates that model-internal representations *can* outperform external embeddings for selection (in the non-iterative setting).\n   - DaaR (NeurIPS 2025) -- uses model's own frozen embeddings for diversity-driven selection; the closest prior work to RGDS in concept.\n   - DiverseEvol (arXiv 2311.08182) -- model-internal Vendi Score for iterative data selection.\n   - DiSF (ICLR 2025 Oral) -- the actual methodological basis for the submodular selection algorithm used in this paper, yet uncited.\n   - Quad (ICLR 2025 Poster) -- joint quality-diversity optimization for data selection.\n   - DCES (Electronics 2026) -- dynamic center-edge sampling specifically for iterative collapse, the most directly comparable baseline.\n\n3. **Concept drift and embedding drift:**\n   - Gama et al. (2014), \"A Survey on Concept Drift Adaptation\" -- the alignment gap is a special case of concept drift.\n   - Lazaridou et al. (2021), \"Mind the Gap: Assessing Temporal Generalization in Neural Language Models\" -- temporal drift in language model representations.\n   - Luo et al. (2024) on embedding drift in continual learning settings.\n\n4. **Active learning under distribution shift:**\n   - The alignment gap is structurally identical to the well-known problem in active learning where the acquisition function (here: diversity score) becomes miscalibrated as the model updates. Works by Ash et al. (2020, BADGE), Kirsch et al. (2019), and the broader Bayesian active learning literature on representation shift are relevant.\n\n5. **Self-play and RLHF feedback loops:**\n   - Constitutional AI (Bai et al. 2022) and iterative RLHF involve similar feedback loops where model outputs influence future training. The connection to reward hacking / reward model overoptimization (Gao et al. 2023) -- where the model exploits misalignment between proxy and true objectives -- is structurally analogous to the alignment gap.\n\n6. **Representation collapse in contrastive/self-supervised learning:**\n   - \"Better Fine-Tuning by Reducing Representational Collapse\" (ICLR 2021) -- directly studies representation collapse during fine-tuning.\n   - Jing et al. (2022), \"Understanding Dimensional Collapse in Contrastive Self-Supervised Learning\" -- theoretical framework for why representations collapse.\n\n7. **Diversity metrics:**\n   - Quality-Weighted Vendi Score (ICML 2024), DCScore (ICML 2025) -- modern diversity measurement methods that the paper should position against.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 3.0/5\n- Excitement: 3.0/5\n- Overall: 2.5/5\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Justification\n\nThe paper identifies a real and practically relevant phenomenon -- the misalignment between internal embedding diversity and content diversity during iterative self-training. The selection ratio metric is a clean contribution, and the experimental design (multiple seeds, scales, datasets) is appropriate. However, the paper has significant weaknesses from a novelty and related work perspective that prevent recommendation for acceptance in its current form. The related work coverage is inadequate for EMNLP: only 10 references for a paper sitting at the intersection of three active research areas (model collapse, data selection, representation quality) is well below venue expectations. Critical prior work is missing, most notably DiSF (the actual algorithmic basis), DaaR and MONA (model-internal diversity selection), DCES (iterative collapse mitigation), self-verification (NeurIPS 2025), and the concept drift literature. The novelty claim is overstated: that internal representations become unreliable under distributional shift is conceptually expected; the quantification is the true contribution but is framed as if the direction itself is surprising. The neural collapse connection is under-supported. The \"best case\" effect sizes (ratio 1.04->0.52, PPL +50%) appear to exceed what the current experimental evidence supports -- the median scenario (ratio 1.03->0.78, PPL +13%) is more credible. At its current state, this is a borderline Findings paper that needs (1) substantially expanded related work, (2) more careful calibration of novelty claims, (3) verification that all reported numbers are from completed experiments, and (4) engagement with the concept drift and active learning literatures that provide the natural theoretical home for this observation.","project":"phase-gated-compound-sparse","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260513T081019","overall_score":"borderline findings"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":691,"cache_read_input_tokens":67543,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":691,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VK81W9kNp4nJVM3Zcv39zM","session_id":"7a73def8-44d6-41cf-95fb-f642ea1414d1","uuid":"12daf35c-c2a2-44e2-beec-3b71c520e99e"}
